4.931259844909164e-09, 1.3404554799478774e-08], [4.4560383685166016e-05, 5.609324489341816e-07, 5.912184519729635e-08, 3.4022272643596807e-07], [6.1549071688205e-05, 3.4723607313935645e-06, 8.329760021297261e-06, 1.8586208625492873e-06], [2.994357782881707e-05, 2.785186552500818e-06, 6.681315426249057e-06, 8.578978849982377e-06], [8.749720592504673e-08, 1.2730768617075228e-07, 2.840618407162765e-08, 9.595233859727159e-05], [6.204730743775144e-05, 3.966551958001219e-06, 1.2877509334430215e-06, 1.002901399260736e-06], [2.9034927138127387e-06, 7.892511348472908e-06, 8.943387001636438e-06, 9.615047747502103e-05]], "new_cot": "1. Deforestation refers to the removal or clearing of forests, usually for agricultural purposes or urbanization.\n2. Increased insect populations (A) are not directly related to deforestation. While insects may be affected by changes in their habitat, they do not cause deforestation.\n3. Clearing for farming (B) is a primary reason for deforestation. As human populations grow, more land is needed for agriculture, leading to the clearing of forests.\n4. Reduction in rainfall (C) can contribute to deforestation indirectly. Droughts and reduced rainfall can weaken trees and make them more susceptible to diseases and pests, which may lead to their death and removal. However, this is not the primary cause of deforestation.\n5. More carbon dioxide (D) is a consequence of deforestation rather than a cause. Trees absorb carbon dioxide during photosynthesis, so when they are removed, there are fewer trees to absorb this greenhouse gas.", "new_cot_probs": [1.1627113855050553e-10, 4.2224446588079445e-06, 4.058261227068982e-10, 2.1507998507197534e-11], "cot_prob": [-49.75], "cot_step_prob": [-49.75]}, "2": {"completion": "B): Clearing for farming.\n\n", "probs": [7.581033023562611e-10, 1.669833727646619e-05, 1.8185952743721145e-09, 3.5810265863744917e-10], "prediction": 1, "target_cot_step": "Deforestation refers to the removal of trees and vegetation from an